tests/kernel/inode_lifecycle.py and vfs_semantics.py exercise extracted production
functions with controlled kernel primitives. They do not establish Linux ABI,
device correctness, or SELinux behaviour on the supported kernel builds.

Selective fixes reviewed on 2026-10-17 against the 2026-10-16 report:

- dbd7f4a5cc375bea0f5b9203669fcc9114c8588a: retain GET_UIDS wire cursors as
  u32 and reject empty/nonpositive counts before indexing. Cursors at or beyond
  count, including 0x80000000 and UINT32_MAX, return an empty page unchanged.
- dacb65546736132f388e79841a4451fe64442936: prepare both separate HM inode/file
  operation copies and all superblock/xattr allocations before publishing any
  child. Propagate ENOMEM/unsupported-hook errors to the existing rule rollback
  and first-failure cursor protocol. Real-parent paths are retained only after
  successful child publication. Existing compatible hooks can be reused.
- 7f227863c5ca98c6c349e8f362f6419221074968 / 5f13384ffb98bcb92d2abf03c741b6a17b60e0a6 /
  9ea42b0b1c6a088d40adee510c4c56f341de1a25: read proxy pointers under RCU
  from entry. Directory topology has an explicit ownership ref and sleeping
  lookup/iterate readers take independent refs before leaving RCU. Resolver
  snapshots also pin their target directory across inode disposal/retry. The
  existing inode-generation count and orphan-slot retirement still decide when
  topology ownership ends; ART, seqcount, SRCU and opaque semantics remain.
  Revalidation samples all proxy data under RCU, then drops it before calling
  the original filesystem callback. Dentry hook installation protects its first
  proxy read as well.
- 7a24793f727e4d4fcf35e549f64827604b8f3a44: dynamic directory fops use
  THIS_MODULE and independently retain their original filesystem module owner.
  Open files keep their f_op after inode restoration, so HM retains all copied
  directory fops until module exit, clearing their directory pointers on inode
  teardown. Exit releases each original owner after the existing RCU barrier.
  This deliberately retains one fop allocation and filesystem-owner reference
  per hooked inode generation until unload; inode churn can grow this memory.
  It is not a proof of safe unload for pre-hook FDs or every VFS callback.
- e6756af82652dbaa6a905673d592afb6dcaebcef: capture fsuid once per directory
  iteration and pass it through actor filtering and virtual-child emission.
  The fixed 64-bit Bloom and tagged-single fast path are retained; adaptive
  Bloom sizing is not ported without a measured production implementation.

A local review also identified cached inodes surviving same-flag rule replacement
with an obsolete backing path. Rule snapshots now carry borrowed backing identity
for comparison under RCU; both cached lookup generations and revalidation compare
flags plus backing dentry/mount, while owned path references remain unchanged.

Host regressions in tests/kernel additionally cover hostile UID cursors, every
hook allocation/module-owner failure point, RCU entry snapshots, owned topology
refs spanning sleeping callbacks, and one fsuid read across multiple actor calls.
They compile extracted production functions against controlled kernel primitives;
these checks do not substitute for GKI/DDK builds or KASAN/lockdep device testing.
No prebuilt kernel object is regenerated by this selective source port.

Local boundary fixes following the 2026-10-17 review:
- Native directory cursor IDs remain unsigned through array bounds checking.
- Directory children retain separate (name, UID) entries. Exact UID selection
  precedes global fallback in lookup and emission, including whiteouts. Parent
  topology selection and generated ancestors carry the requested UID; cached
  inode identity also includes the selected directory topology.
- Plain seqcount writers disable preemption for each publication section.
- Each installed superblock hook retains an active superblock reference while
  the caller still pins its mount, releasing it only after operation restoration.
  Filesystem shutdown may therefore be delayed until Hybrid Mount unloads even
  after its rules have been cleared and its mount has been detached.
The host fixtures now exercise these boundaries and paired teardown references.
The 16-bit compat cursor still limits representable child positions; enumerating
65,536 or more children needs a separate cursor protocol change.

